WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.367
Ray.

2
00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:03.927
Some jobs are too big for one machine.

3
00:00:03.927 --> 00:00:06.900
Ray lets you spread plain Python across a cluster.

4
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.500
Ray makes a cluster feel like one big Python process.

5
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:17.200
Decorate a function with ray remote, and it becomes a task that can run on any free worker.

6
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.433
The scheduler handles placement, data movement, and fault tolerance.

7
00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:24.000
Let's follow one call.

8
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.200
Calling dot remote returns a future immediately, without waiting.

9
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.200
The scheduler sends the work to free workers anywhere in the cluster.

10
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:33.833
They all run in parallel.

11
00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:36.567
And ray get collects the results when you need them.

12
00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:39.367
Ray has two building blocks.

13
00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:41.601
A task is a remote function.

14
00:00:41.601 --> 00:00:43.467
An actor is a remote class.

15
00:00:43.767 --> 00:00:45.071
A task is stateless.

16
00:00:45.071 --> 00:00:48.333
An actor is a stateful worker you call methods on.

17
00:00:48.633 --> 00:00:54.033
Think of a task as embedding one batch, and an actor as a model held in GPU memory.

18
00:00:54.333 --> 00:00:57.100
Either way, the cluster feels like one process.

19
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.233
On this base, Ray ships higher level libraries.

20
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:03.033
At the bottom, Ray core.

21
00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:05.633
Data does distributed preprocessing.

22
00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:07.833
Train does distributed training.

23
00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:11.908
And Serve handles model serving with autoscaling.

24
00:01:11.908 --> 00:01:15.067
vLLM even uses Ray for multi GPU serving.

25
00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:17.467
Here it is in code.

26
00:01:17.767 --> 00:01:20.600
We decorate a normal function with ray remote.

27
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:24.700
Calling dot remote on every batch returns futures instantly.

28
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.800
Then ray get waits and collects all the vectors.

29
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.433
The loop body never changed.

30
00:01:30.633 --> 00:01:31.867
Now a real job.

31
00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:35.866
A new embedding model means re-embedding two million chunks.

32
00:01:35.866 --> 00:01:37.900
Serially, that is fourteen hours.

33
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.667
With Ray, the batches fan out across an eight node spot cluster.

34
00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:47.267
It finishes in under two hours, and Ray retries any batches lost when a node was reclaimed.

35
00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:49.241
Let's recap.

36
00:01:49.241 --> 00:01:52.533
Ray remote turns function calls into cluster tasks.

37
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:56.100
You get futures right away, and collect them with ray get.

38
00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.100
Actors are stateful workers.

39
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.942
And Serve, Train, Data, and RLlib are built on top.

40
00:02:01.942 --> 00:02:04.233
Next up: Triton Inference Server.
